# Append to /etc/fstab. Requires `[automount] enabled=false` + `mountFsTab=true`
# in /etc/wsl.conf (see files/wsl.conf).
#
# --- Windows fixed drives -----------------------------------------------------
# Mount ONLY the fixed drives. Every other drive letter (USB, card reader) is
# deliberately left unmounted so Windows can always eject it: WSL keeps an
# auto-mounted volume open, and the only way to release it is `wsl --shutdown`.
#
# These are mounted rw on purpose. An earlier revision of this config appended
# `ro` to lock WSL out of the Windows filesystem as a guard against an AI agent
# running a stray `rm -rf`. That was dropped: `ro` is bypassed by the very
# powershell() bridge in ~/.shell_wslfn that we deliberately keep, so it never
# stopped an agent -- it only made ordinary Windows-side commands fail with
# confusing EROFS errors. Accident protection is git commit discipline instead.
#
# Adjust the drive list to the machine. To reach a removable drive ad hoc,
# without rebooting:
#   sudo mkdir -p /mnt/f && sudo mount -t drvfs 'F:' /mnt/f -o metadata,uid=1000,gid=1000,umask=22
#   sudo umount /mnt/f        # run this BEFORE ejecting from Windows
#
# The mount points must already exist; with automount disabled WSL will not
# create them for you:
#   sudo mkdir -p /mnt/c /mnt/d /mnt/e
C:	/mnt/c	drvfs	metadata,uid=1000,gid=1000,umask=22	0 0
D:	/mnt/d	drvfs	metadata,uid=1000,gid=1000,umask=22	0 0
E:	/mnt/e	drvfs	metadata,uid=1000,gid=1000,umask=22	0 0
